    agent: "knox",
    stage: "extract",
    lesson: "A count of free uses before a price is an allowance, not \"no fee\": \"3 free per month (then $1.50 per)\", \"First 5 pages free, then $0.50 per page\", \"4 sheets free (then $2.00 per sheet)\" and \"Counter Check (3 free then $0.25/check)\" each charge the price after the allowance for every later use. Read that price under the name before the allowance, waivable (only that fee, not the row's other fees), at exactly the frequency the same fee gets with no allowance: rebuild the row as it would print without the allowance and read it with the same readers, rather than keeping a separate list of units (\"then $25.00 hourly\" is hourly, and the next fee's \"Annual Fee\" is never this price's period), whatever period the allowance counts in (\"per month\" is how often the free uses renew, not how often the fee is charged). Read it only when \"then\" (or a dash before an \"after\" price) joins the allowance to the price in the same cell. Leave everything else as no fee: a free stretch of time, its time word before or after \"free\" (\"First 6 months free, then $5.00 per month\", \"2 free months\", \"6 free statement cycles\", \"first 1 hour free\"), is an introductory offer; a figure a deposit, balance, minimum, limit or requirement follows (\"then $500 balance required\") is a condition; a rate after it (\"then 1%\") is a rate; other free or no-fee words on the row (\"incoming free\", \"no fee for Premier Checking\") waive something else; an allowance line with no name of its own never takes the fee on the line above; and an ATM allowance whose own words name no other ATMs (\"ATM Transaction Fee | 5 Free Transactions per month, then $1.00\") can be the bank's own machines; only the name, the allowance and the clause right after the price count, never a bracketed note or another clause (\"$1.00 at our ATMs; $2.00 at other ATMs\"). Never let the allowance's price go to another fee on the same row.",
