    lesson: "A list that prints each price first and its name after a dash (\"$25.00 - NSF Check/ACH/ATM item\", \"$20.00 - Stop Payment (single check)\", \"$35.00 - Stop Payment (range of checks)\") prices each line's own fee: never give a line's price to the name on the line above, or every fee in the list takes the next fee's price. Read such a run as the label rows it lists (\"NSF Check/ACH/ATM item | $25.00\") so the name, category, frequency and audience are exactly what a label row gets (\"(per hour)\" stays hourly), rather than writing new rules for the dash. Read it that way only where the reading cannot be anything else: the words after the dash must be a name (not a condition, sentence, lone period, range, count or a clause; \"$10.00 - If both keys are lost, ...\" is still the price of \"Replace Lost Key\" above it), the lines must be a run of two or more that no bare price closes, the line above must not be a fee's name that could own the first price, every row read must trace to the stored text as Darwin will check it, and no other read may be lost. Otherwise read the text as stored. A new reading that would replace a read that publishes today is a reject and a reprice: keep base's read and only add new fees until a live re-check and its dry-run count exist, because a fresh read of the same document publishes beside the old row, not instead of it.",
