    id: "backlog-dry-run-7-2026-11-03-whose-charge-the-name-says",
    agent: "knox",
    stage: "extract",
    lesson: "File a charge by whose cost its own name says it is, and read the name as the bank prints it, not as a footnote number or note leaves it. Verifying a borrower's employment or income is a loan's underwriting cost, never an account verification. A statement fee whose name opens with a credit card is the card account's, never the deposit paper statement, and must not stand in for the deposit fee printed on the same page. A footnote number glued to a name's last word (\"Overdraft Transfer2,10\") is not part of the word: classify the name without it, so two readers of one line agree and one row is kept. A starred waiver note in the name's own cell ends with that cell and never covers the next cell's price, and a note that e-statements are free is how to avoid the fee, not a free price. A qualifier after a dash that names no account, audience, fee or rate (\"Revolution Money Market - New Money Only*\") is a note on the account name before it. An insufficient item the bank pays, naming no return, is the overdraft, not the NSF row. When a narrower rule is added, check that the line's other readings still hold: freeing a name from its footnote must not hand \"Copy of Paper Statement\" to the paper statement rule.",
