    id: "backlog-dry-run-9-2026-11-03-a-row-names-its-own-fee",
    agent: "knox",
    stage: "extract",
    lesson: "A price takes its fee from its own row, never from a word its family rule or its heading would like it to be. A handling service for returned items (special handling, a re-clear, a reroute, a service fee priced per month) is a service, not the charge for the item, so it is held unless its name says NSF (\"(Special Handling)\" in a bracket after the charge is only a note); an automatic transfer from shares or savings is the overdraft protection transfer on its own name, whatever heading it sits under. When the account is titled in the first cell of a two-column line right above the fee, that title heads the fee in that column and no other. A reward bullet (\"Waived $5.00 monthly maintenance fee\") or a \"Your $12 monthly maintenance fee ...\" sentence names no account, so hold it rather than publish it accountless, unless the page prints that fee at that price as a row of its own (then read it from that row) or names the account right above the sentence; a FAQ's second sentence about one free service is the same $0, and a bracket the name never closes is no part of it. Waiver advice that offers several ways is held, but advice whose every choice is the same fee's kind (\"Use Non-Network ATM or Foreign ATM\") is that fee. Before reading a name by its family or heading, check whether its own words already say what it is.",
