    id: "backlog-dry-run-10-2026-11-03-the-line-must-say-it",
    agent: "knox",
    stage: "extract",
    lesson: "Read a price only from words the page prints for it. A heading that names an audience by age or stage of life (\"Senior Perks (60 & Over)\", \"Members 65+\") makes its rows that audience's prices, so carry the heading as the product and never let the audience's price stand for the general one; a note that exempts an audience, a quantity (\"Over 10,000\", \"Over 12 Months\") and an account's own title (\"Student Checking\", \"Kids Club\") are not such headings. A line that lists the channels a fee covers (\"Includes checks, ACH, ATM ...\") names no fee, so it never chooses the category of the row under it. A dollar figure joined to a percentage by its bound words (\"2% minimum or $2\") is the rate's minimum or maximum, never a flat fee. A $0 needs its own line, or a price-only cell right above it, to print it (\"$0\", \"None\", \"Free\", \"No charge\", an unconditional \"Waived Annual IRA Fee\"); a waiver on a condition (\"waived when the minimum balance is met\"), another perk's label above, or a \"$0 or $4.95\" offer is no free price. A marketing sentence about an account is not the account's title, and a label full of control characters is no title at all. Before keeping a read, find the words on the page that say this price, for this fee, for this customer.",
